WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.160
With the Disney+ debut of The&nbsp;
Falcon and the Winter Soldier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:09.680
Sam Wilson is set to take on a leading role in&nbsp;
the MCU — so there's no better time to find out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:14.240
a little more about his comic book history.&nbsp;
This is the untold truth of the Falcon.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:22.080
The Falcon first debuted in Captain America 117,&nbsp;
which was published all the way back in 1969. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.680
this comic, Sam Wilson is a resident of Harlem&nbsp;
who discovers that a group of Hydra agents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:33.360
led by the Red Skull, are operating out of a&nbsp;
tropical island called Exile. Wilson and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:39.280
trained falcon, Redwing, soon discover the group's&nbsp;
nefarious plan, and along the way he learns that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:44.960
they've been enslaving indigenous tribes on the&nbsp;
island. Sam is then encouraged by Captain America&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:51.360
himself to adopt a superhero moniker of his own&nbsp;
and save the islanders from the Red Skull's men.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:57.360
Artist Gene Colan had the idea for the Falcon&nbsp;
partly as a response to the civil rights movement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.880
and, for a number of reasons, he's generally&nbsp;
regarded as one of the more empowering characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:09.280
of the Silver Age of Comics. For one, he's&nbsp;
arguably the first mainstream Black hero to not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:15.200
actually have the word "Black" in his name. Falcon&nbsp;
was also considered an equal to Captain America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.040
rather than a sidekick, and the two&nbsp;
famously received co-billing in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.480
ongoing Captain America series&nbsp;
— for almost a hundred issues.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.920
Despite receiving co-billing with Cap, however,&nbsp;
it took a long time for Falcon to get a chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:34.000
to shine in his own series. Indeed, his origin&nbsp;
story has been retconned several times, and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.440
like many other comic book heroes, he's bounced&nbsp;
between several different teams over the years.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:46.160
Falcon finally appeared as a solo act in a 1979&nbsp;
issue of Marvel Premiere, a "tryout" series that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:50.880
the company used to gauge interest in new&nbsp;
material, although it didn't lead to much.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:56.480
Four years later, he led a four-issue miniseries&nbsp;
that identified Sam Wilson as a mutant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:00.480
but that plot twist was wisely retconned in 2001.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:05.520
It wasn't until 2017, almost 50&nbsp;
years after the character's debut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:10.560
that the Falcon got his own true solo&nbsp;
series: the sensibly titled Falcon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:16.720
led by writer Rodney Barnes and artist Joshua&nbsp;
Cassara. However, as Marvel fans will know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:21.840
Wilson led his own series a few years before&nbsp;
that — but it wasn't as the Falcon at all.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:28.400
Starting in 2014, Sam Wilson replaced his original&nbsp;
partner, Steve Rogers, as Captain America.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:34.320
The transition occurs after Rogers has the&nbsp;
Super-Soldier Serum drained from his body during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:40.880
a battle with Iron Nail, which forces him to hang&nbsp;
up the shield as he rapidly ages into an old man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:44.000
Considering how long the&nbsp;
two had fought side by side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.120
Wilson was a natural fit to take over the&nbsp;
role and continue Captain America's legacy.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:53.600
This makes the ending of Avengers: Endgame&nbsp;
even more significant to longtime fans of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:59.040
the comics — since both Bucky Barnes and Sam&nbsp;
Wilson have taken up the shield in the comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.560
it really could have been either of&nbsp;
them that filled Steve Rogers' shoes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:07.840
But while Rogers and Wilson do share a knack for&nbsp;
heroism, it will be interesting to see how the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:12.880
Falcon's totally different backstory meshes&nbsp;
with the role of Captain America in the MCU.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:18.560
For his MCU appearances, Falcon was given&nbsp;
a handful of technological upgrades that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:24.000
fit with a more modern interpretation of the&nbsp;
character. In his original iterations, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:29.680
he was pretty much just a dude in a costume. As&nbsp;
his character started to gain a bit more traction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:34.560
certain elements were added: for example,&nbsp;
miniature solar arrays were inlaid into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:38.400
the fiber, which allowed his suit to&nbsp;
essentially provide its own power.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.000
In both the movies and later&nbsp;
iterations of the comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.000
Falcon pilots a Stark Industries&nbsp;
drone, codenamed "Redwing."

00:03:46.608 --> 00:03:49.057
"Payload secure. Thanks, Sam."

00:03:49.057 --> 00:03:50.720
"Don't thank me."

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:52.080
"I'm not thanking that thing."

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:53.360
"His name is Redwing."

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:54.640
"I'm still not thanking it."

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:55.760
"He's cute. Go on, pet him."

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.360
That's a far cry from his early years,&nbsp;
though, when he was partnered with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:04.960
actual trained falcon named Redwing. Falcon's&nbsp;
origin involves him forging a connection with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:09.440
the bird, who's been with the character ever&nbsp;
since that first issue of Captain America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:14.320
and the two share a bond that goes far beyond&nbsp;
the emotional. It seems only right, then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:19.040
that Redwing stays by Falcon's side even&nbsp;
when he takes up Captain America's shield.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:23.440
Falcon has gone through a handful of other&nbsp;
technological upgrades over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:28.240
too. His first modern costume was&nbsp;
created at Captain America's request&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:34.480
not long after he made his debut, and it was even&nbsp;
made by a familiar face: King T'Challa of Wakanda.

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:39.120
Originally, Black Panther designed Falcon's&nbsp;
wings to have just a few basic features,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:44.400
including the solar receptors, jet boosters,&nbsp;
and a small handful of other tricks and gizmos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.400
Over time, however, Falcon has had&nbsp;
T'Challa — and a few others — upgrade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:53.360
his suit to give it the versatility and&nbsp;
power to rival Marvel's slickest heroes.

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:58.560
One more recent iteration of the costume&nbsp;
features holographic wings made of "hard light,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:04.160
which feature a maximum wingspan of fifty feet.&nbsp;
The wings can shield him from enemy firepower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:07.680
they're virtually indestructible,&nbsp;
and they can be manipulated into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:11.440
variety of shapes and configurations&nbsp;
at the drop of a hat. All of this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:16.720
controlled through a cybernetic connection run&nbsp;
through Falcon's mask directly into his brain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:21.440
meaning the whole system is controlled by his&nbsp;
thoughts. Quite a leap from wings made of fabric.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:23.840
"The receipt for your gear."

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:27.280
"Bird costume? Come on!"

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:27.920
"I didn't write it."

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:32.240
Falcon's origin story has been&nbsp;
changed a few different times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:37.360
but one thing has remained constant: he was&nbsp;
originally a relatively ordinary man with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:42.720
no superpowers to speak of. Still, when he&nbsp;
fights alongside Redwing in his debut story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:46.880
the connection between Wilson and his&nbsp;
avian ally seems abnormally strong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:52.080
But it later turns out that the Red Skull&nbsp;
himself helped give this power to Wilson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:58.240
as part of a plot that revolved around a very&nbsp;
familiar Marvel MacGuffin — the Cosmic Cube.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:03.040
In a later story, it's revealed that the&nbsp;
Red Skull used the Cosmic Cube to brainwash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:08.160
Wilson into turning against Captain America&nbsp;
— an act which also established a telepathic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:12.640
link between the Falcon and Redwing.&nbsp;
As he grows into his role as Falcon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:18.560
Wilson learns to expand this link, and what&nbsp;
begins merely as a means of communication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:21.680
soon becomes the ability to&nbsp;
see through Redwing's eyes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:26.960
Eventually, Wilson becomes able to see through&nbsp;
any bird's eyes, and even access their memories.

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:30.240
But the Falcon's encounter with&nbsp;
the Cosmic Cube had other side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:34.400
effects as well — one of which would&nbsp;
lead to a very unfortunate retcon.

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:40.000
The confusing nature of comic books can mean&nbsp;
some characters are given many back stories.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:45.120
Since so many different writers are involved in&nbsp;
such a wide, shared universe, some pretty strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:50.560
contradictions and retcons can emerge if you go&nbsp;
back far enough. And despite Falcon enjoying a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:56.240
pretty strong debut, his character was later given&nbsp;
a new — and immensely disappointing — origin.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:02.160
In his original story, Falcon is a social worker&nbsp;
from Harlem. That background was overturned in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:07.920
later run, however, in which it is revealed that&nbsp;
Sam Wilson's true identity is "Snap" Wilson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:12.160
a Harlem mobster and pimp, who was&nbsp;
essentially the epitome of an ugly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:17.200
racial stereotype. His social worker origin&nbsp;
is explained to have been a false memory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:21.920
implanted in Wilson's mind as a side&nbsp;
effect of the Cosmic Cube's powers.

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:27.920
Fast forward a few decades to 2015, and it&nbsp;
turns out that the "Snap" Wilson origin was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:33.360
the actual false memory, implanted by Red&nbsp;
Skull to sow confusion in the Falcon's mind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:37.360
Wilson's life as a social worker is&nbsp;
subsequently revealed to be the real memory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:40.240
having simply been overwritten&nbsp;
by the power of the Cube.

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:46.080
Comics love to critique, satirize, and subvert&nbsp;
things that are happening in the real world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:50.240
and Falcon has tackled his fair&nbsp;
share of real world issues, too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:56.560
One of the strangest instances of this occurred&nbsp;
during a brief Avengers run that began in 1979,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:00.240
when Wilson joined the team —&nbsp;
and quit almost immediately.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:06.400
At this point in time, the United Nations liaison&nbsp;
for the Avengers is a man named Henry Gyrich,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:11.200
who tasks Captain America with recruiting&nbsp;
Falcon to join the team. However, Falcon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:17.680
learns that Gyrich has merely tapped him to fill&nbsp;
a racial hiring quota, and he isn't best pleased.

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:21.120
Falcon has come and gone from a&nbsp;
variety of teams over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:25.440
but he has almost always stayed true&nbsp;
to his convictions. As a character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:30.320
he believes in being recognized for a person's&nbsp;
skills and abilities, and his principle in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:35.040
quitting a group like the Avengers due to&nbsp;
being a "token" hire epitomizes that belief.

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:40.320
In many ways, Sam Wilson was the one who&nbsp;
helped Steve Rogers heal from the grief of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:44.560
losing his old partner, Bucky Barnes. When&nbsp;
it is revealed that Bucky has been alive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:50.400
all along — albeit having been brainwashed&nbsp;
into becoming a Soviet assassin — Wilson is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:55.520
there for Rogers as they try to help Barnes&nbsp;
regain his memories. Their dynamic in the MCU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.520 --> 00:09:00.400
is a little different than in the comics,&nbsp;
as, in the movies, the Falcon and Captain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:05.040
America don't have half a century's worth&nbsp;
of history together. In the comics, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:10.640
a mutual respect is earned between Sam and Bucky&nbsp;
— not just because of their relationship with Cap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:15.280
but because they both spent time carrying&nbsp;
the shield when Rogers stepped aside.

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:19.760
There weren't many storylines that really&nbsp;
explored this relationship properly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:25.600
though — until the 2020 Falcon &amp; Winter Soldier&nbsp;
series, that is. When the series picks up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:30.240
Falcon is back in the bird-suit and Bucky has&nbsp;
received a pardon for his murderous past — but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:35.680
it's contingent on working for an agency that&nbsp;
locates and eliminates domestic terrorists.

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:40.160
The series forces Wilson and Barnes&nbsp;
together as they try to track down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:45.040
Baron Zemo — as well as a handful of other&nbsp;
villains, including the Natural, a psychotic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:50.560
teenage assassin with a Captain America fetish.&nbsp;
Over time, the series deepens their friendship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:55.840
and allows both to evolve as heroes — much like&nbsp;
the upcoming Disney+ series is poised to do.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:00.960
Not too much is known about the Disney+&nbsp;
Falcon and Winter Soldier series so far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:04.960
as Marvel and Disney have been typically&nbsp;
tight-lipped about what to expect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:07.440
But one or two details are certain, at least.

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:12.720
"Oh yeah, I can see it working. Gears turning&nbsp;
— oh, they're malfunctioning. They're on fire!"

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:14.880
"God, I hate you."

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:19.520
Most importantly, both Anthony Mackie and&nbsp;
Sebastian Stan will be continuing in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:26.000
respective roles of Sam Wilson and Bucky Barnes,&nbsp;
joined by returning MCU stars Emily VanCamp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:31.200
who plays Sharon Carter, and Daniel&nbsp;
Brühl as the nefarious Baron Zemo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:35.360
Joining the cast is Wyatt Russell, who&nbsp;
is taking on the role of John Walker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:38.560
the U.S. government-approved&nbsp;
replacement for Steve Rogers.

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:43.360
With Wilson having been handed Captain America's&nbsp;
shield at the end of Avengers: Endgame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:47.680
it seems certain that the show will focus&nbsp;
on Wilson's transition from the Falcon to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:52.000
the new Captain America. The series was&nbsp;
created and written by Malcolm Spellman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:55.840
who is best known for writing several&nbsp;
episodes of the TV series Empire.

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:00.240
Very few details are known about The&nbsp;
Falcon and the Winter Soldier's plot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:04.240
but it isn't all that hard to figure out&nbsp;
which comic books have inspired the series'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:08.640
story — especially considering where the&nbsp;
characters ended up after Avengers: Endgame.

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:13.680
The most likely route for the show to take would&nbsp;
involve borrowing beats from the Captain America:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:18.960
Sam Wilson run. In 2015, Captain America&nbsp;
fought that battle with the Iron Nail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:23.360
which led to his rapid aging and subsequent&nbsp;
retirement from his life as a superhero.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:28.640
Rogers then passes the mantle to Sam Wilson, who&nbsp;
has to cope with the responsibilities of becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:33.200
Captain America. In light of the similar&nbsp;
circumstances at the conclusion of Endgame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:37.040
this storyline seems a likely&nbsp;
influence on the series.

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:41.440
Beyond that, though, anything could happen. But&nbsp;
considering the involvement of so many villains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:47.840
and anti-heroes — including Baron Zemo, John&nbsp;
Walker, Batroc the Leaper, and Flag-Smasher — you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:53.440
can be sure of at least one thing: Sam Wilson&nbsp;
and Bucky Barnes are gonna have their hands full.

